  "analysis": "Environment: Debian 11, qemu-system-x86_64 and expect are available, but no sshpass. /app has alpine.iso (1GB) and an empty-ish alpine-disk.qcow2. I need to boot the ISO in qemu, get a shell on the VM (via serial console), set root password to password123, bring up networking, and start sshd with port 2222 forwarded to the guest's port 22. First I must determine how the ISO behaves: does it output to a serial console by default, and what kernel/initrd/cmdline it uses. I'll inspect the ISO's boot config. I also need to know what connection tools are available (nc, socat) to drive the serial console via expect.",
  "plan": "1) Check for nc/socat/python3 and mounting tools. 2) Mount the ISO read-only and inspect its syslinux/isolinux config and boot directory to find vmlinuz, initramfs, and the APPEND line (especially whether console=ttyS0 is set). This determines whether I can just boot with -cdrom/-nographic, or must use -kernel/-initrd/-append to force a serial console. Based on that I'll construct the qemu launch and an expect script.",
